-- title:
--   Isotropic submodules of a free rank-two module are cyclic
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a field and $T$ a commutative $K$-algebra that is finite-dimensional over $K$ and reduced. Let $V$ be an abelian group carrying compatible $K$- and $T$-module structures (a scalar tower $K \to T \to V$), and suppose given a $T$-linear isomorphism $e \colon V \xrightarrow{\ \sim\ } (\mathrm{Fin}\ 2 \to T)$, so that $V$ is free of rank two over $T$ with a chosen basis. Let $B \colon V \to V \to K$ be a $K$-bilinear form which is left-nondegenerate, in the sense that any $x \in V$ with $B(x,y) = 0$ for all $y \in V$ is zero, and which is $T$-balanced, meaning $B(t \cdot x, y) = B(x, t \cdot y)$ for all $t \in T$ and $x,y \in V$. Let $N$ be a $T$-submodule of $V$ which is isotropic for $B$: $B(x,y) = 0$ whenever $x, y \in N$. The conclusion is that there exists $g \in V$ with $N = \operatorname{span}_T \{g\}$; that is, $N$ is a cyclic $T$-module (the generator $g$ is produced in $V$, and membership in $N$ is not separately asserted, though it follows from the equality).
--
--   This is the linear-algebra input behind the cyclicity of the part of a $\mathfrak{P}$-adic Tate module cut out by inertia: a submodule of a rank-two module over a reduced finite-dimensional Hecke algebra which is isotropic for a balanced nondegenerate pairing must be generated by a single element. It is used in [`ModularCurve.exists_generator_tateModule_closure_inertia_smul_sub_adjoin_tateHeckeRep`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_generator_tateModule_closure_inertia_smul_sub_adjoin_tateHeckeRep).
